1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,854
An engineer runs git pull on Monday morning
and gets this line, with a wall of hints

2
00:00:09,894 --> 00:00:15,248
above it and exit status 128. They post in
the team channel: "pull is broken, is the

3
00:00:15,288 --> 00:00:16,083
server down?"

4
00:00:16,423 --> 00:00:19,316
The server is fine. The
network transfer succeeded.

5
00:00:19,356 --> 00:00:22,996
Something in their repository did
change, and something did not.

6
00:00:23,036 --> 00:00:27,597
If you can say which is which, and name
the three possible answers and what each

7
00:00:27,637 --> 00:00:32,197
does to history, you are answering one of
this course's interview questions, and

8
00:00:32,237 --> 00:00:36,855
that line stops looking like an error.
Hold on to the question: what changed, and

9
00:00:36,895 --> 00:00:37,603
what did not?

10
00:00:39,443 --> 00:00:43,108
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:43,148 --> 00:00:48,089
Two topics today, and they belong together
because the second one reads the first.

12
00:00:48,429 --> 00:00:53,043
The first is the upstream of a branch: two
lines of configuration that tell status

13
00:00:53,083 --> 00:00:57,698
what to compare with, pull what to integrate,
and, under the default setting, push

14
00:00:57,738 --> 00:01:01,841
where to go. Remember that a remote
is your repository's name for another

15
00:01:01,881 --> 00:01:06,439
repository, and that origin/main records
where the server's main was at your last

16
00:01:06,479 --> 00:01:11,036
fetch. You will set an upstream in three
different ways and watch a bare git push

17
00:01:11,076 --> 00:01:12,909
refuse for two different reasons.

18
00:01:13,249 --> 00:01:17,975
The second is git pull. You already know
both of its halves: fetch, from the last

19
00:01:18,015 --> 00:01:22,742
video, which downloads commits and never
moves your own branches, and merge, from

20
00:01:22,782 --> 00:01:26,508
the module before, which joins
another history into your branch.

21
00:01:26,548 --> 00:01:31,392
Today you watch them run one after the
other, see where the seam is, and learn what

22
00:01:31,432 --> 00:01:36,276
decides whether the second half is a
fast-forward, a merge, a rebase, or a refusal.

23
00:01:38,116 --> 00:01:39,694
Here's what you'll walk away with.

24
00:01:40,034 --> 00:01:41,446
After this video you can:

25
00:01:41,786 --> 00:01:46,937
Explain what branch.<name>.remote and
branch.<name>.merge record, and set an

26
00:01:46,977 --> 00:01:48,303
upstream three ways.

27
00:01:48,643 --> 00:01:53,013
Predict where a plain git push
goes under push.default=simple.

28
00:01:53,353 --> 00:01:56,575
Describe git pull as fetch
followed by merge or rebase.

29
00:01:56,915 --> 00:02:01,506
Explain the "divergent branches" error and
the three configurations that answer it.

30
00:02:03,346 --> 00:02:07,902
The upstream. In one sentence: the upstream
of a local branch is the branch on a

31
00:02:07,942 --> 00:02:11,693
remote that status compares it with
and that pull integrates from.

32
00:02:11,733 --> 00:02:13,532
It's two lines of configuration.

33
00:02:13,872 --> 00:02:17,442
Quick quiz. One of those
two lines names the remote.

34
00:02:17,482 --> 00:02:22,441
Does the other one hold, A, origin/main,
your remote-tracking ref, or B,

35
00:02:22,481 --> 00:02:26,190
refs/heads/main, the server's
own name for the branch?

36
00:02:26,230 --> 00:02:27,023
Your answer?

37
00:02:31,863 --> 00:02:35,315
It's B. Precisely:
branch.<name>.remote names a remote.

38
00:02:35,355 --> 00:02:39,886
branch.<name>.merge names a ref on
that remote, such as refs/heads/main.

39
00:02:39,926 --> 00:02:44,965
Note what kind of name that is: the server's
name, not your remote-tracking ref.

40
00:02:45,005 --> 00:02:50,107
If you picked A, you're in good company.
The shorthand <branch>@{upstream}, short

41
00:02:50,147 --> 00:02:54,551
@{u}, resolves the pair to the
remote-tracking branch that records it.

42
00:02:54,891 --> 00:02:59,777
Three commands read it. git status and
git branch -vv use it for the ahead and

43
00:02:59,817 --> 00:03:03,188
behind counts. git pull uses
it for what to integrate.

44
00:03:03,228 --> 00:03:07,356
git push uses it for where to push
under the default push.default.

45
00:03:07,696 --> 00:03:13,146
Inside .git: only .git/config. An
upstream is not a ref and not an object.

46
00:03:13,486 --> 00:03:19,001
push.default decides what a bare git push,
with no remote and no refspec, updates.

47
00:03:19,041 --> 00:03:23,674
There are five values, and the demo
runs all of them from one branch.

48
00:03:23,714 --> 00:03:26,113
The default since Git 2.0 is simple.

49
00:03:26,453 --> 00:03:31,227
Pull. In one sentence: git pull 🟡 CAUTION
is git fetch followed by one integration

50
00:03:31,267 --> 00:03:35,748
of the fetched upstream into the current
branch: a fast-forward, a merge or a

51
00:03:35,788 --> 00:03:39,799
rebase. When the two branches have
diverged, Git makes you say which.

52
00:03:40,139 --> 00:03:45,055
Precisely. Step 1 runs git fetch with the
same arguments, so remote-tracking refs

53
00:03:45,095 --> 00:03:46,707
and FETCH_HEAD are updated.

54
00:03:47,047 --> 00:03:50,999
Step 2 integrates the FETCH_HEAD
line that is marked for merging.

55
00:03:51,039 --> 00:03:55,912
If your branch is an ancestor of it, the
branch is fast-forwarded, as on screen.

56
00:03:55,952 --> 00:03:59,597
If it is an ancestor of your
branch, there is nothing to do.

57
00:03:59,637 --> 00:04:04,572
If neither is true the branches have
diverged, and pull.rebase, pull.ff or a flag

58
00:04:04,612 --> 00:04:05,985
must select the method.

59
00:04:06,325 --> 00:04:11,364
Inside .git: everything a fetch changes,
then everything a merge or a rebase

60
00:04:11,404 --> 00:04:16,912
changes. The branch ref, the reflogs, whose
entries begin with pull, ORIG_HEAD, the

61
00:04:16,952 --> 00:04:18,717
index and the working tree.

62
00:04:19,057 --> 00:04:23,787
When not to pull? On a machine
that should never create commits.

63
00:04:23,827 --> 00:04:29,825
And the failure mode is not the fatal
message. That message is Git behaving well.

64
00:04:29,865 --> 00:04:35,937
The failure mode is the older behavior,
which the textbook's version note records.

65
00:04:35,977 --> 00:04:41,453
Before Git 2.27 an unconfigured pull
on diverged branches merged silently.

66
00:04:41,493 --> 00:04:47,117
From 2.27 to 2.33.0 it merged with a
warning. Since Git 2.33.1 and 2.34.0 it

67
00:04:47,157 --> 00:04:49,726
fetches, prints the hint and stops.

68
00:04:51,566 --> 00:04:56,593
A picture helps. For the upstream, the
textbook's analogy is a default correspondent

69
00:04:56,633 --> 00:05:00,695
printed on a letter template, so
that pull and push need no address.

70
00:05:00,735 --> 00:05:05,098
It breaks because incoming and outgoing
mail can have different defaults:

71
00:05:05,138 --> 00:05:09,743
@{upstream} and @{push} are two questions.
With one remote they have the same

72
00:05:09,783 --> 00:05:12,940
answer. A later video shows
a setup where they don't.

73
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:17,721
For pull, the analogy is "collect the mail
and file it" as a single instruction.

74
00:05:17,761 --> 00:05:22,426
It breaks when filing needs a decision: if
both sides have new commits there are two

75
00:05:22,466 --> 00:05:27,131
legitimate ways to combine them, they produce
different histories, and pull will not

76
00:05:27,171 --> 00:05:27,971
choose for you.

77
00:05:28,311 --> 00:05:31,662
So read every pull output as
two outputs glued together.

78
00:05:31,702 --> 00:05:35,294
Find the seam. Above it is the
fetch, which cannot hurt you.

79
00:05:35,334 --> 00:05:40,320
Below it is a local integration, which is
a merge or a rebase that you already know

80
00:05:40,360 --> 00:05:42,560
how to reason about, and how to undo.

81
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:50,802
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on paper. You
and Asha each added one commit on top of

82
00:05:50,842 --> 00:05:57,323
ef22149: yours is c498de8, hers is 527a708.
Draw that, then what a merge adds, then

83
00:05:57,363 --> 00:06:02,036
what a rebase leaves. How many
new commits does each create?

84
00:06:02,076 --> 00:06:03,293
Say it out loud.

85
00:06:08,133 --> 00:06:14,129
Same input, three outcomes. Here's the
input, after the fetch half: your main has

86
00:06:14,169 --> 00:06:18,674
c498de8, origin/main has 527a708,
and both grow from ef22149.

87
00:06:19,014 --> 00:06:24,652
The merge keeps c498de8 and adds a
commit with two parents, 7044e06.

88
00:06:24,992 --> 00:06:31,066
The rebase replaces c498de8 with a new
commit, 45eec24, on top of origin/main.

89
00:06:31,106 --> 00:06:36,318
So each answer creates one new
commit, of two very different kinds.

90
00:06:36,358 --> 00:06:38,042
And --ff-only refuses.

91
00:06:39,882 --> 00:06:45,277
Part 1: no upstream. A push asks the server
to move one of its refs to a commit of

92
00:06:45,317 --> 00:06:49,916
yours. You create a branch, commit,
and run a bare git push 🟡 CAUTION.

93
00:06:49,956 --> 00:06:51,573
Predict. Say it out loud.

94
00:06:56,413 --> 00:07:02,020
git branch -vv shows main with [origin/main]
in brackets and the new branch with

95
00:07:02,060 --> 00:07:07,950
nothing. The push refuses to guess, exit
status 128, and tells you two ways forward.

96
00:07:08,290 --> 00:07:12,572
-u, long form --set-upstream, pushes
and then writes the two keys.

97
00:07:12,612 --> 00:07:18,008
Look at the value of merge:
refs/heads/feature/eval-harness, the name of the branch

98
00:07:18,048 --> 00:07:18,925
on the remote.

99
00:07:19,265 --> 00:07:24,276
@{u} resolves to the remote-tracking ref.
@{push} is where git push would send the

100
00:07:24,316 --> 00:07:27,787
branch. With one remote it's
the same ref. git log @{u}..

101
00:07:27,827 --> 00:07:32,839
lists what you have not pushed, and git
log ..@{u} lists what you have fetched and

102
00:07:32,879 --> 00:07:36,412
not integrated. Those two are
worth an alias in your head.

103
00:07:36,752 --> 00:07:39,245
Part 2: the other ways
an upstream appears.

104
00:07:39,585 --> 00:07:44,242
Switching to a name that exists only as a
remote-tracking branch creates the local

105
00:07:44,282 --> 00:07:47,965
branch with its upstream. That is
the guess from the clone video.

106
00:07:48,305 --> 00:07:53,635
git branch -u 🟡 sets an upstream for an
existing branch without pushing, and

107
00:07:53,675 --> 00:07:59,358
--unset-upstream removes it. Watch git
status -sb: with an upstream, the line has

108
00:07:59,398 --> 00:08:03,527
three dots and a second name.
Without one, only the branch.

109
00:08:03,867 --> 00:08:09,305
With push.autoSetupRemote=true, a bare push
of a branch that has no upstream creates

110
00:08:09,345 --> 00:08:12,371
the branch on the server
and sets the upstream.

111
00:08:12,711 --> 00:08:18,285
Part 3: push.default. You create latency-fix
from origin/main, commit, and push.

112
00:08:18,325 --> 00:08:23,337
Predict: the branch has an upstream
this time. Does the push go through?

113
00:08:28,177 --> 00:08:33,149
It refuses again, for a different reason.
latency-fix was created from origin/main,

114
00:08:33,189 --> 00:08:37,980
so automatic setup made origin/main its
upstream. Under the default, simple, Git

115
00:08:38,020 --> 00:08:42,931
refuses to push when the upstream's name
differs from the branch's name: it cannot

116
00:08:42,971 --> 00:08:46,614
know whether you mean "update
main" or "publish latency-fix".

117
00:08:46,954 --> 00:08:53,160
The other values each answer that question.
-c push.default=<value> with --dry-run

118
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:55,978
shows them without changing anything.

119
00:08:56,318 --> 00:09:00,905
upstream: latency-fix -> main, your
commit would go to main on the server.

120
00:09:00,945 --> 00:09:04,156
current: a new branch
latency-fix. nothing: refuses.

121
00:09:04,196 --> 00:09:09,158
And matching: read that line again. It
pushed feature/eval-harness, a branch you

122
00:09:09,198 --> 00:09:12,472
were not on, and did not
push the branch you were on.

123
00:09:12,512 --> 00:09:17,348
matching was the default before Git 2.0.
That line is why the default changed.

124
00:09:17,688 --> 00:09:23,556
Part 4: pull, the fast-forward
case. labs/run ch12/pull-anatomy.

125
00:09:23,596 --> 00:09:25,125
No local commits.

126
00:09:25,465 --> 00:09:29,613
Find the seam. The From block
is the fetch. Updating ...

127
00:09:29,653 --> 00:09:33,277
Fast-forward is the merge.
Both reflogs say pull.

128
00:09:33,617 --> 00:09:39,536
Part 5: diverged. Asha has pushed "Raise
top_k to 8", and you have committed "Add

129
00:09:39,576 --> 00:09:44,391
dev requirements". Status says
[ahead 1], which is the stale view.

130
00:09:44,731 --> 00:09:50,751
The first two lines are the fetch half,
and it worked: ef22149..527a708 main ->

131
00:09:50,791 --> 00:09:56,811
origin/main. Then the hint and the fatal
line. Now the question from the start.

132
00:09:56,851 --> 00:10:02,027
Predict: what does git status -sb
say now, and did your branch move?

133
00:10:02,067 --> 00:10:04,098
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

134
00:10:08,938 --> 00:10:14,105
[ahead 1, behind 1]. A pull that
ends in this error is not a no-op.

135
00:10:14,445 --> 00:10:17,971
Your remote-tracking ref moved,
and status now tells the truth.

136
00:10:18,011 --> 00:10:22,668
Your branch, index and working tree are
untouched. The fatal error is not a network

137
00:10:22,708 --> 00:10:27,253
failure and not damage. Git fetched
successfully and then declined to pick one of

138
00:10:27,293 --> 00:10:29,403
two possible histories on your behalf.

139
00:10:29,743 --> 00:10:32,932
The hint offers three
answers. Each one as a flag.

140
00:10:33,272 --> 00:10:38,941
--ff-only, configuration pull.ff=only,
means "move my branch only when there is

141
00:10:38,981 --> 00:10:44,940
nothing to combine". On diverged branches
it refuses, with a different message from

142
00:10:44,980 --> 00:10:46,530
the unconfigured case.

143
00:10:46,870 --> 00:10:53,117
--no-rebase, configuration pull.rebase=false,
creates a merge commit with two

144
00:10:53,157 --> 00:10:59,812
parents, 7044e06. Its message contains the
URL because pull merges FETCH_HEAD, not

145
00:10:59,852 --> 00:11:00,792
origin/main.

146
00:11:01,132 --> 00:11:05,823
To show the third answer from the same
state, the demo takes the merge back.

147
00:11:05,863 --> 00:11:10,928
git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD 🔴 DANGEROUS.
ORIG_HEAD holds the commit HEAD pointed at

148
00:11:10,968 --> 00:11:16,095
before the pull. The five answers: it moves
the branch and overwrites the index and

149
00:11:16,135 --> 00:11:17,154
the working tree.

150
00:11:17,494 --> 00:11:22,253
It can destroy uncommitted changes. Preview
with git status and by checking what

151
00:11:22,293 --> 00:11:25,973
ORIG_HEAD names. Recovery of the
commit is through the reflog.

152
00:11:26,013 --> 00:11:30,893
Uncommitted changes are not recoverable
that way. It is appropriate to take back a

153
00:11:30,933 --> 00:11:35,333
pull you did not want, on a clean tree.
Chapter 11 has the full treatment.

154
00:11:39,673 --> 00:11:45,502
--rebase, configuration pull.rebase=true,
re-creates your commit on top of the

155
00:11:45,542 --> 00:11:51,672
upstream: c498de8 became 45eec24. History
stays linear, and the old commit remains

156
00:11:51,712 --> 00:11:57,692
reachable from the reflog. Read the reflog
from the bottom: start, pick, finish.

157
00:11:58,032 --> 00:12:03,422
Part 6: which setting wins. labs/run
ch12/pull-matrix runs git pull from one

158
00:12:03,462 --> 00:12:08,351
diverged state under every combination
of the two configuration keys.

159
00:12:14,891 --> 00:12:21,670
Three rules, as observed on Git 2.55.0.
With nothing set, a diverged pull is fatal.

160
00:12:21,710 --> 00:12:27,667
pull.ff=only in configuration beats
pull.rebase in configuration: the row

161
00:12:27,707 --> 00:12:33,828
pull.rebase=true pull.ff=only refuses.
A flag on the command line beats all

162
00:12:33,868 --> 00:12:39,168
configuration: each flag gave the
same result in all twelve rows.

163
00:12:41,008 --> 00:12:42,656
Five mistakes to watch for.

164
00:12:42,996 --> 00:12:46,484
Reading the "divergent branches"
message as a failed pull.

165
00:12:46,524 --> 00:12:51,534
Root cause: the fetch half succeeded and
moved origin/main; Git stopped before step

166
00:12:51,574 --> 00:12:55,428
2 because two histories are possible
and nothing was configured.

167
00:12:55,768 --> 00:13:00,781
Creating a topic branch from origin/main
and running a bare git push with

168
00:13:00,821 --> 00:13:06,388
push.default=upstream. Root cause: automatic
setup made origin/main the upstream,

169
00:13:06,428 --> 00:13:08,188
and upstream pushes to it.

170
00:13:08,528 --> 00:13:14,075
Thinking branch.<name>.merge names origin/main.
Root cause: it stores the server's

171
00:13:14,115 --> 00:13:19,527
ref name, refs/heads/main; @{u} is what
resolves the pair to the remote-tracking

172
00:13:19,567 --> 00:13:19,800
ref.

173
00:13:20,140 --> 00:13:26,382
Setting pull.rebase=true and pull.ff=only
together and expecting a rebase.

174
00:13:26,422 --> 00:13:33,344
Root cause: in configuration, pull.ff=only
beats pull.rebase, as the matrix shows.

175
00:13:33,684 --> 00:13:38,741
Letting a deploy host run a plain git pull.
Root cause: on divergence a configured

176
00:13:38,781 --> 00:13:43,215
host merges or rebases and so creates
a commit that exists nowhere else.

177
00:13:45,055 --> 00:13:50,546
Now, out of the lab. An ML engineer
branches exp/lr-sweep from origin/main and

178
00:13:50,586 --> 00:13:56,431
commits a sweep configuration. If their
configuration says push.default=upstream, a

179
00:13:56,471 --> 00:14:00,048
bare git push sends those
commits straight to main.

180
00:14:00,088 --> 00:14:01,537
With simple it stops.

181
00:14:01,877 --> 00:14:06,021
The textbook gives two clean
setups for topic branches.

182
00:14:06,061 --> 00:14:12,412
One: branch.autoSetupMerge=simple, which
sets an upstream only when the names match,

183
00:14:12,452 --> 00:14:18,270
and needs Git 2.37 or later. Two: git
switch -c <name> --no-track origin/main

184
00:14:18,310 --> 00:14:20,932
together with push.autoSetupRemote.

185
00:14:21,272 --> 00:14:25,680
And for pull: a deploy host or a CI
job should never create commits.

186
00:14:25,720 --> 00:14:31,174
Its update step is git pull --ff-only, or
git fetch followed by an explicit checkout

187
00:14:31,214 --> 00:14:36,537
of the fetched commit. A host that merges
on pull will one day build a commit that

188
00:14:36,577 --> 00:14:37,846
exists nowhere else.

189
00:14:38,186 --> 00:14:41,832
On choosing between the two
integrations for yourself.

190
00:14:41,872 --> 00:14:47,430
Rebase keeps history linear, but every
replayed commit is a new commit that nobody

191
00:14:47,470 --> 00:14:52,960
has tested, and local merge commits are
flattened unless you use --rebase=merges.

192
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:58,081
Merge preserves exactly what you tested
and adds one merge commit per pull.

193
00:14:58,121 --> 00:15:03,747
The textbook calls a global pull.ff=only a
defensible default: pull never creates a

194
00:15:03,787 --> 00:15:07,980
commit unless you pass --rebase
or --no-rebase at that moment.

195
